  m_Name: English
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  language: English
  inputText:
  - Look at the cubes to make them light up!
  - "HERE. Black in Rembrandt\u2019s time"
  - "Black people were already here, in the Netherlands, in the seventeenth century,
    in Dutch society and in art.  Rembrandt regularly portrayed black people, but
    how are black people represented in the rest of seventeenth-century Dutch art?
    What is the image projected? And who were the models?  A relatively large number
    of black people appeared in art, particularly between 1620 and 1660. They are
    shown in all kinds of roles and often accurately portrayed from life. It has
    recently been discovered that a significant community of black people lived in
    the neighbourhood of Rembrandt\u2019s own house. This unusual period in Dutch
    art and history is the subject of this temporary exhibition."
  - 'Black from a distance '
  - "Map of Pernambuco, in: Johannes Blaeu, \u2018Grooten Atlas oft, Werelt-beschrijving\u2019,
    Amsterdam 1665 "
  - 'A map of the Dutch colony in the northeast of Brazil. This is how the colony
    was depicted. Upper right there is a sugar mill where enslaved Africans worked.
    There is no hint of the harsh working conditions. To the left the Africans are
    shown dancing and making music. For the people of the Netherlands the gruesome
    reality of slavery was kept out of the picture. '
  - STUDIES
  - Head of a Boy, c. 1660-1665  Leendert van der Cooghen
  - This finely executed study must have been observed directly. The boy looks very
    lifelike. Unfortunately, we know nothing about him. Leendert van der Cooghen
    lived and worked in Haarlem. He made this kind of study to find out how he could
    depict the subject best. It was study material, not intended for sale.
  - Black in Amsterdam
  - The Market in Dam Square, 1653  Jacob van der Ulft
  - "In the seventeenth century, Amsterdam was already a multicultural city. Jewish
    immigrants, East Europeans, Arab traders, Asian seamen and Africans had made
    their homes here. This view of Dam Square offers proof. It shows a group of men
    in eastern turbans slightly to the left of centre. And one black figure, beside
    a fish stall in the foreground on the right. The number of black residents was
    small. Some five thousand people were living in and around the Jodenbreestraat
    around 1650. Around eighty of them were black \u2013 about 1.5 percent of the
    neighbourhood\u2019s residents."
  - Portraits
  - "Few portraits of black people were made in the seventeenth century. That is
    to say images of a particular person where the recognizability of that individual
    is concerned. However, there are portraits of black people, but mostly as servants
    of the white elite. Unfortunately we seldom know the sitter\u2019s identity. "
  - Portrait of Diego Bemba, 1643  Jasper | Jeronimus Becx
  - "We know the names of these three men from the inscriptions on the backs of the
    paintings. This is Diego Bemba. He holds a small casket, probably a diplomatic
    gift. He was one of Don Miguel de Castro\u2019s servants. "
  - Portrait of Dom Miguel de Castro, 1643  Jasper | Jeronimus Becx
  - 'Dom Miguel de Castro makes here a powerful, serious expression. He was a member
    of the Congolese elite. This portrait was commissioned by the Dutch West India
    Company. The WIC and the Congolese rulers traded in gold an ivory, but mainly
    in people. This is why Dom Miguel now is seen as a controversial figure. Dom
    Miguel wears fashionable European clothes. This is probably the suit he had acquired
    in Brazil, when he had visited the Dutch colony earlier. '
  - Portrait of Pedro Sunda, 1643  Jasper | Jeronimus Becx
  - "Pedro Sunda holds an elephant\u2019s tusk. Ivory was one of the Congo\u2019s
    major export products. The status of these young men is unclear. Were they assistants
    of the noble Dom Miguel or were they enslaved? "
  - Young black archer, ca. 1639-1640  Govert Flinck
  - Rembrandt's pupils also painted realistically. This painting by Govert Flinck
    from 1640 may have been modeled on a black man who lived nearby. Flinck carefully
    studied the man's facial expression and gestures in order to paint a fantasy
    figure that he could sell on the market.
  - Woman in her toilet with servant, 1678  Frans van Mieris
  - From 1640 black servants were painted differently, they were given different
    roles. They were now often associated with one main white figure, and they were
    active. They held a horse, or a mirror to assist when a white woman got dressed.
    They were no longer in the front, but to the side or in the background. They
    were smaller than the main character. In this way, the painter could make the
    main figure seem more important.
  - 'Man in a Turban, c. 1627-1629  Jan Lievens '
  - 'Lievens made this drawing in Leiden when he was working closely with his friend
    Rembrandt. The individual appearance makes  it clear that someone modelled for
    this, but that model was then given a turban and a feather to wear, turning him
    into a Mediterranean type. '
  - 'Portrait of a girl, 1645  Wenzel Hollar '
  - 'Three rare portrait etchings of black people. They wear the normal seventeenth-century
    clothing of the middle class. Hollar made these portraits in Antwerp, as we can
    see from the inscription beside the girl. The prints were intended for sale.
    The artist showed his skill by successfully rendering a black skin in an etching.
    This appealed to collectors. '
  - Portrait of a man, c. 1660-1675  Wallerant Vaillant
  - Vaillant made a true likeness of this man with an individual appearance . However,
    this print appears to have been made to sell to collectors, not commissioned
    by the sitter. Vaillant was an expert in mezzotint, a technique for making prints
    in different shades of black. It was obviously also ideal for depicting dark
    skin.
  - Two African Men, 1661  Rembrandt
  - "It seems logical that two black neighbours of Rembrandt were           the models
    for this painting. But the painting is dated 1661,           when Rembrandt was
    living on Rozengracht, on the other side                of Amsterdam. However,
    there is documentary evidence that                         he had already made
    a painting of two black men while living                           in Jodenbreestraat.
    Were there other paintings of this subject?                                            
    Or did Rembrandt only finish off the first painting and date it                                              
    a few years later?  It is also unusual that Rembrandt depicted the close bond 
    between the two men. Were they friends? Or perhaps brothers?               It
    remains speculation, but if the models were brothers, and if                           
    they came from the neighbourhood, they may have been Bastiaan                                            
    and Manuel Fernando. They were sailors from the island of S\xE3o                                                
    Tom\xE9 in their early twenties, who lived at the end of the street                                                       
    near Sint Antoniespoort. "
  - Head of a Boy in a Turban, c. 1635  Gerrit Dou
  - "Dou was Rembrandt\u2019s first pupil. He took up his master\u2019s idea of studying
    black people. The result was this endearing tronie of a young  man in a fantasy
    costume, who looks at us over his shoulder.  "
  - King Caspar, 1654   Hendrick Heerschop
  - "Legend has it that one of the three magi who came to worship the Christ child
    was an African. Sometimes he is called Caspar, sometimes Balthasar. Heerschop
    painted him without surroundings or story. He can only be identified from his
    expensive clothes and the jar of incense he gave as his gift. But it is the man\u2019s
    face that attracts the most attention; he looks at us proudly and self-confidently. "
  - Here and now
  - "Black plays an entirely different role in contemporary art than it did in Rembrandt\u2019s
    time. In seventeenth-century art we see black people through the eyes of white
    artists and we do not know who the models are. Black artists today often reflect
    on their own identity, reality, and surroundings. When black people are portrayed,
    we know who they are. There is agency from both sides now: of the maker and the
    sitter. Even more prominently than in the past, black people are present, here,
    in society and in art. "
  - The Unspoken Truth, 2019  DION ROSINA  Dion Rosina used a nineteenth-century
    photograph of an enslaved man in Brazil for this painting, The opening is an
    allusion to the Netherlands' colonial past in that country -- a subject that
    is a hiatus in our collective memory. The past is behind the opening, but at
    the same time impresses itself on the viewer through the man.
  - 'The New Utopia Begins Here: Hermina Huiswoud, 2019   IRIS KENSMIL   In her portraits,
    Kensmil focuses on black people who played important roles in history. Hermina
    Huiswoud (1905-1998) was a political activist. She belonged to a generation of
    anti-colonial thinkers, leaders and organizers who were active in the fight against
    European and American imperialism. After the Second World War she worked in Amsterdam,
    and as a supporter of the Our Suriname Association (VOS) fought for the independence
    of the Dutch colony. '
  - Ilona, 2019  HEDY TJIN  Hedy Tjin is an illustrative designer. In her work she
    expresses her fascination for patterns and designs, reflecting the influence
    of cultural and ethnic subjects.
  - "Doritos, 2019  IRI\xC9E ZAMBL\xC9  A woman sits on a couch holding a bag of
    tortilla chips. Black people are now represented in art in everyday situations.
    The artist Iri\xE9e Zambl\xE9 expresses her feelings: \u2018I still don't see
    enough of the people who pass me every day reflected in art. That's why I feel
    an urge to capture them in my paintings.\u2019 "
  - "Hollandse Meesters Her-Zien: Debora Nassy, 2020 CIGDEM YUKSEL (fotografie),
    J\xD6RGEN TJON A FONG (concept)  Debora Nassy was a woman of colour who worked
    as a servant in Amsterdam. When she was about to leave for Guyana, she visited
    a notary to make sure that she would not be taken into slavery overseas. Her
    employer, the Jewish-Portuguese David Nassy, declared that Debora \u2013 \u2018a
    brown female or mulatta\u2019 \u2013 was born in freedom in his house and was
    raised as a free person. "
  - Exit
